-- Q10108: Public Company Reporting [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits require retroactive adjustment of all share data. Pre-split incremental shares: 10,000 - (10,000 x $60/$80) = 2,500; dilution ratio = 2,500/200,000 = 1.25%. Post-split: 20,000 - (20,000 x $30/$40) = 5,000; ratio = 5,000/400,000 = 1.25%. The proportional dilution is unchanged.
Wrong (A): While incremental shares double to 5,000, the total share base also doubles to 400,000, so the dilution percentage remains the same.
Wrong (B): The split does not eliminate dilutive effect; both exercise price and market price adjust proportionally, maintaining the in-the-money relationship.
Wrong (D): Stock option terms are adjusted for stock splits under ASC 260-10-55-12, so incremental shares do not remain at 2,500.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10108;

-- Q10131: Public Company Reporting [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes exercise proceeds repurchase shares at the average market price. Year 1: 50,000 - (50,000 x $38/$40) = 2,500 incremental shares. Year 2: 50,000 - (50,000 x $38/$60) = 18,333 incremental shares. Higher market price means fewer shares repurchased, increasing dilution.
Wrong (B): A higher market price increases dilution because the fixed exercise proceeds buy back fewer shares at the higher price, not more.
Wrong (C): The exercise price and option count staying constant does not prevent changes in dilution; the market price variable directly affects the treasury stock calculation.
Wrong (D): Options remain dilutive when the market price exceeds the exercise price; a wider spread increases dilution rather than causing antidilution.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10131;

-- Q10175: Public Company Reporting [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 260-10-45-10, share repurchases reduce weighted-average shares outstanding from the repurchase date. With constant net income in the numerator and fewer shares in the denominator, basic EPS increases.
Wrong (B): Share repurchases are treasury stock transactions affecting equity, not the income statement. The cash outflow does not reduce net income.
Wrong (C): Treasury stock directly reduces the weighted-average share count used in the EPS denominator, so it does affect the EPS formula.
Wrong (D): Repurchasing shares does not create a gain in the numerator. Under U.S. GAAP, treasury stock transactions are equity transactions with no income statement effect.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10175;

-- Q10205: Public Company Reporting [medium/L2]
