  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options in the money (exercise price > market price) are dilutive. Using the reverse treasury stock method: proceeds = 15,000 x $50 = $750,000; shares at market = $750,000 / $40 = 18,750; incremental shares = 18,750 - 15,000 = 3,750 added to the denominator.
Wrong (A): Written puts are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds market price, unlike call options where that relationship makes them antidilutive.
Wrong (B): Simply adding all 15,000 shares ignores the reverse treasury stock method calculation required by ASC 260.
